==================== SECURITY REVIEWER ====================

## Security & Privacy Review – Password‑Reset Flow

| Category | Risk | Impact | Likely Cause | Mitigation / Recommendation |
|----------|------|--------|--------------|-----------------------------|
| **Conflicting / Vague Requirements** | Two expiration windows (10 min + 15 min) | Inconsistent behavior → potential security loophole | Ambiguous wording | Clarify with business: choose a single, unambiguous expiry (e.g., “15 minutes after issuance”). |
| | Password policy “subject to change” | Weak passwords → account compromise | Undefined constraints | Document current policy, expose it via a policy‑service API, and design tests to be policy‑agnostic. |
| | “Temporarily locked” definition | Unclear when reset is allowed | Undefined lock state | Define lock duration, lock reason, and how it interacts with reset logic. |
| | Success‑message timing | UI may reveal whether email exists | Timing side‑channel | Show generic “If an account exists, a reset link has been sent” message. |
| | Token security specifics | Token may be guessable or replayable | No size/algorithm defined | Use a 128‑bit (≥32‑char) cryptographically random token, store a hash, and transmit over HTTPS only. |
| | Email content | Phishing risk, token exposure | Plain‑text email | Use secure email templates, avoid embedding the token in the subject, and consider a “click‑through” link that redirects to a secure page. |
| | Account lock handling | Reset may bypass lock → account takeover | Business rule | Ensure lock status is checked *after* token validation but *before* password change; log the event. |
| | Time‑synchronization | Premature expiry or never‑expire | Server clock drift | Use NTP, store timestamps in UTC, and validate against server time. |
| | Token revocation on account deletion/deactivation | Token may still be usable | No revocation logic | Invalidate or delete token when account is deleted or deactivated. |
| | Token in URL | URL may be logged, cached, or leaked | Browser history, server logs | Encode token in a POST form or use a short‑lived, single‑use link; avoid storing token in logs. |
| | Session handling after reset | Old sessions may remain valid → session fixation | No session invalidation | Invalidate all existing sessions on password change; issue a new session cookie with `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Strict`. |
| | Error messages | Information leakage (e.g., “account not found”) | Detailed error responses | Return generic “Invalid or expired link” for all failure cases. |
| | Rate‑limiting | Brute‑force token guessing | No throttling | Limit reset requests per IP/account (e.g., 5 per hour). |
| | Audit trail | Lack of forensic evidence | No logging | Log token generation, usage, expiry, failures, and account lock status changes. |
| | Email delivery failure | User may not receive link → denial of service | Email service outage | Retry mechanism, fallback to SMS/phone if available, and notify user of delivery status. |
| | Token storage | Plain‑text token in DB → data breach | No hashing | Store a salted hash of the token; compare hashes on validation. |
| | Token length | Short token → brute‑force | No minimum length | Enforce ≥128‑bit entropy (≥32 ASCII chars). |
| | CSRF protection | Reset form may be hijacked | No CSRF token | Require a CSRF token on the reset form. |
| | Privacy of email address | Email address may be exposed in logs | Logging raw request data | Mask or hash email addresses in logs; avoid logging the full address. |
| | GDPR / CCPA compliance | Personal data handling | No data‑retention policy | Retain token only until expiry or use; delete after use. |
| | Session fixation | New session may be hijacked | No session regeneration | Regenerate session ID after password reset. |
| | Token leakage via browser history | Token may be stored in history | URL usage | Prefer POST or short‑lived link; advise users to delete history. |
| | Token replay after expiry | Replay attack if token not invalidated | No single‑use enforcement | Mark token as used immediately after first successful reset. |
| | Email content privacy | Email may be intercepted | No encryption | Use TLS for email transport (STARTTLS) and consider PGP/

==================== QA REVIEWER ====================

**REVISE**

The test set covers most of the functional requirements, but several critical edge‑cases and business‑risk scenarios are missing or duplicated:

| Issue | Why it matters | Suggested addition |
|-------|----------------|--------------------|
| **Ambiguous expiry rule** | Two conflicting times (10 min vs 15 min) are tested separately (TC004 & TC005). Until the rule is clarified, the tests should be consolidated or marked as “pending clarification.” | Add a note that the test will be updated once the expiry policy is confirmed. |
| **Rate‑limiting** | Brute‑force or abuse of the reset endpoint can lead to denial‑of‑service or account compromise. | Add a test that verifies the system limits reset requests per IP/account. |
| **Session handling** | After a password reset, existing sessions may remain valid, exposing the account to session‑fixation attacks. | Add a test that ensures all active sessions are invalidated and a new session is issued. |
| **CSRF protection** | The reset form could be hijacked if no CSRF token is required. | Add a test that verifies a CSRF token is required on the reset form. |
| **Generic error messages** | Detailed errors (“Account not found”, “Link already used”) can leak information. | Add a test that verifies all failure paths return a generic “Invalid or expired link” message. |
| **Audit trail** | Lack of logging makes forensic investigation difficult. | Add a test that verifies token generation, usage, expiry, and failures are logged. |
| **Success‑message wording** | The requirement specifies “immediately after request submission,” but the wording could reveal whether an account exists. | Add a test that verifies the success message is generic (e.g., “If an account exists, a reset link has been sent”). |

Once these gaps are addressed, the test suite will provide comprehensive coverage of both functional and security‑critical aspects of the password‑reset flow.

